<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiralal Shastri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiralal Shastri was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> activist and social reformer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, founder of the Vanasthali Vidyapith women's college. In 1949 he became the first chief minister of the United State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, leading the elected ministry that governed the union of former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, and remained in office until 1951.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hiralal Shastri was born on 24 November 1899 into a peasant family at Jobner in Jaipur District, where he received his early education. He passed the degree of Sahitya Shastri in 1920 and in 1921 stood first in the B.A. examination at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>'s College, Jaipur. The same year he joined the Jaipur State Service, rising to become Secretary in the Home and Foreign Departments, but he resigned in 1927 to work in the countryside, as he had wished to since childhood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1929 he chose the remote village of Banasthali, 72 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, and founded Jeevan Kutir there, where he trained social workers and pursued a programme of rural reconstruction. These workers later helped spread political awakening in many of the Rajputana States. On 6 October 1935 he and Ratan Shastri, with the help of fellow freedom fighters such as Durgadutt Harit, founded Banasthali Vidyapith in memory of Shantabai, his daughter from his first marriage, who had died very young.</p><p>He joined the Jaipur Rajya Praja Mandal in 1937, was twice its general secretary and twice its president, and in 1939 led its satyagraha for civil liberties, for which he was imprisoned for six months. In 1947 he became general secretary of the All India States Peoples Conference and was elected to the Constituent Assembly. When representative government was installed in Jaipur State on 27 March 1948 he became its prime minister, and on the formation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> on 30 March 1949 its first chief minister, working to bring the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> into a single modern administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shastri resigned as chief minister on 5 January 1951 and later sat in the Second Lok Sabha. He was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. He died on 28 December 1974, and in November 1976 the Indian Posts and Telegraphs Department issued a commemorative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postage-stamp">postage stamp</a> in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hiralal Shastri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…ministers drawn from the popular movements: Gokul Lal Asawa headed the first government at Kota, Manikya Lal Varma the government formed at Udaipur, and <b>Hiralal Shastri</b> of the Jaipur Praja Mandal became the first premier of Greater Rajasthan in 1949, though the transitional governments were not yet elected.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jobner, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.97,75.38&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Jobner on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hiralal Shastri"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3898 5188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3898 5188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.97,75.38&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
